8.	For the following water quality parameters provide a brief definition of the parameter and units, the equipment used, the purpose of the test, and the measurement procedure for the following water quality parameters: 

Maps to purpose of water test, equipment used, methods and procedures, including, KE3.1 temperature (in situ), KE3.2 pH, KE3.3 Electrical conductivity, KE3.4 Dissolved Oxygen, KE3.5 Turbidity
	S	NS
		
Temperature
Definition & Units: a measure of how hot or cold the water is at the time of measurement. Its units are degrees Celsius  
Equipment Used: Thermometer or multiparameter water quality sonde
Purpose of test: Fundamental physical property of water that is critical for understanding other water quality parameters, It also influences species environmental tolerances, distributions, and behaviour. 
Measurement procedure: responses will vary depending on equipment 
Thermometer: submerge thermometer bulb into water sample and allow to acclimatize, read intersection of the internal fluid with the visible scale. 
multiparameter water quality sonde with temperature sensor turn meter on and lower probe/sensor into the water until sensors are completely submerged allow readings to stabilise and either log or record value.  
pH 
Definition & Units: a measure of how acid or alkaline the water is at the time of measurement on a scale from 0-14 where value below 7 are acidic, 7 is neutral and above 7 is alkaline.  pH is a unitless scale.  
TECH answer it is the negative logarithm of the concentration of hydrogen ions (H+) ie the power of hydrogen 
Equipment Used: pH test strips, pH test liquids, pH digital meters 
Purpose of test: pH measures the acidity or alkalinity of water, which is important for chemical equilibrium, biological activity, and solubility of compounds. The pH of water affects the behaviour of heavy metals and nutrients, and is critical for maintaining a healthy aquatic environment
Measurement procedure: responses will vary depending on equipment and test methodology
pH meters (glass electrode electrochemical potential difference in millivolts pH mV) turn meter remove glass electrode cap if present, and lower probe/sensor into the water until sensors are completely submerged. Allow readings to stabilise and either log or record value once stable.  
pH Indicators such as test strips (Colorimetric method) add prescribed amount of indicator liquid and compare against colorimetric chart
Titrations (Colorimetric method) add prescribed acid volume and neutral indicator to sample volume, titrate and record required volume of sodium hydroxide require to make solution neutral.  
electrical conductivity in water 
Definition & Units: is a measure of the waters ability to conduct electricity units are The units of conductivity are Siemens per meter (S/m), or more commonly milliSiemens per meter (mS/m) or sometimes microsiemens per centimeter (µS/cm)
Equipment Used: conductivity sensor
Purpose of test: it is used to determine the salinity of a water sample. It indicates the level of ions in water and is a proxy for total dissolved solids (TDS)
Measurement procedure: multiparameter water quality sonde with conductivity sensor turn meter on and lower probe/sensor into the water until sensors are completely submerged allow readings to stabilise and either log or record value.  
Dissolved Oxygen (DO)
Definition & Units: is a measure of the amount of soluble oxygen gas is present in the water.  Common units for DO include (milligrams of oxygen per liter (mg/L), parts per million (ppm) and % saturation (% sat)
Equipment Used: multiparameter water quality sonde with a DO sensor
Purpose of test: it is used to determine the level of oxygen gas in the water that is available to aquatic organisms. 
Measurement procedure: multiparameter water quality sonde with conductivity sensor turn meter on and lower probe/sensor into the water until sensors are completely submerged allow readings to stabilise and either log or record value.  
Turbidity 
Definition & Units: Turbidity measures the clarity of water and is influenced by suspended solids like silt, clay, organic matter, and microscopic organisms. High turbidity can affect aquatic life, reduce light penetration, and indicate potential pollution sources.
Equipment Used: multiparameter water quality sonde with a turbidity sensor, Secchi disk, Turbidity tube 
Purpose of test: Turbidity measures the clarity of water and is influenced by suspended solids like silt, clay, organic matter, and microscopic organisms. High turbidity can affect aquatic life, reduce light penetration, and indicate potential eutrophication, microbial blooms and or pollution sources.
Measurement procedure: Responses may vary 
Multiparameter water quality sonde with turbidity sensor conductivity sensor turn meter on and lower probe/sensor into the water until sensors are completely submerged. Do not disturb sediments. Allow readings to stabilise and either log or record value. 

Secchi disk: slowly lower disk into water column until disk disappears visually and record its depth (the length of submerged string from surface to disk). 

Turbidity tube: fill the tube up with the sample water and look directly down the tube and slowly add the sample water in the tube until the marks on the base are no longer visible. Read the NTU scale off the side at the height of the water sample in the tube. The tube needs to be kept clean to allow light to penetrate the water sample. Clean, rinse and dry after use.   
 

